jmp-ai-music-player (1.2.6-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tracks blend at the join instead of cutting.  An mpv audio filter
    (lavfi=[afade=…]) takes the last seconds of every track down and the first
    fraction of the next one up; because mpv already plays consecutive tracks
    without a gap, the two touch and the join reads as a soft dip.  It is a
    playback filter exactly like the loudness levelling — no audio file, tag or
    playlist is rewritten, and switching it off restores the untouched sound.
    lib/chat/fade.py builds the filter (pure, no mpv) and
    Engine._apply_audio_filter carries it together with the levelling gain,
    because mpv has a single `af` property; `music play` builds the same filter
    so a command-line play sounds like a click in the window.
  * Two new settings in the Playback group: Soft endings (fade_songs, on) and
    Fade length (fade_seconds, 1–10 s, default 3).  Both are reachable from the
    chat: "turn the fading off", "enable soft endings", "set the fade length to
    6", "fade the songs out over 5 seconds", "ausblenden nach 4 sekunden", "how
    long is the fade".  A number in a fade sentence sets the length and turns
    the fading on.
  * The out-fade is capped at a third of the track (and a track shorter than
    2.5 s gets none), so a short interlude is never swallowed; the engine waits
    for the next track when the playhead is already inside the fade window
    rather than cutting the ending in.  An mpv build without lavfi/afade
    degrades to levelling only instead of failing to play.
  * Honest limit, written down rather than glossed over: this is a dip, not an
    overlapping crossfade — mpv has none — and the in-fade of an auto-advanced
    track is corrected by the two-second status poll.
  * New headless checks: check_fade covers the filter graph, the cap, `inside`,
    the chaining with the gain, the settings parse and the chat sentences.

 -- Julian Kunkel <julian@kunkel.de>  Thu, 26 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.2.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Capability questions are answered instead of searched.  "can you play spc
    files?", "do you support flac", "is sid supported", "what formats can you
    play", "kannst du spc abspielen" and "spielt der player ogg dateien" used to
    reach the play rule, which stripped the word *play* and looked for a title
    called "can you spc files" — the answer was "Nothing matches".  A format is
    a property of the player, so a new table in lib/chat/formats.py answers it:
    what mpv/ffmpeg can decode, what the library indexes but cannot play
    (.sid, .ym, .play), and what plays but carries no tag block (SPC and the
    tracker modules), with the library's own counts.  A question that names a
    performer ("can you play some jazz") and a command ("play spc") still play.
  * Every reply now says which planner answered.  A chat turn under the local
    planner shows *local planner*, a model turn shows *language model · model ·
    provider · tools*, in the window under the bubble, in the TUI in italics and
    in the plain REPL as [label].  That is what makes the planner order visible:
    offline parser first, then a taught phrase, then the language model.
  * A dead end is handed to the model.  When the offline plan matches but finds
    nothing — "Nothing matches …" from a play, "No tracks match …" from a
    search, an empty mood selection — and a language model is configured, the
    model is asked after all instead of the chat stopping at a hint; without a
    model the hint stays.  `/llm` still prints the full planner state.
  * The language-model tool surface gained `describe_formats` with the same
    answer, so an attached model cannot invent a different one.
  * New headless checks: check_formats, the play and search dead-end rescue
    and the planner labels in check_agent, and the planner field of
    /api/chat.

